In this lesson, we'll look at enlargement, a transformation that makes a shape bigger or smaller while keeping it looking the same. Here's the big idea: every single point of the original shape slides along a straight ray coming out from a special point called the centre of enlargement, and it lands at a new spot that's some multiple of its original distance. That multiple is called the scale factor, usually written as k. So the recipe is simple. You pick a centre point, you pick a scale factor, then for every point in the original, you move it along the ray from the centre through that point by a distance equal to the scale factor times the original distance. What's neat is that the shape itself stays exactly the same, just resized, so all the angles and the orientation are preserved when k is positive. What changes is every length gets multiplied by the scale factor, every area gets multiplied by the scale factor squared, and every volume gets multiplied by the scale factor cubed. One more thing, if k is positive the new point sits on the same side of the centre as the original, but if k is negative it flips to the opposite side. Now let's see it in action.